WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.040
You import a Grok Bot template.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.840
The role sounds useful.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.920
Then you ask it to keep you updated.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.480
Updated about what? From which sources?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.840
And when is it finished?

00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:13.120
Before browsing templates, name the result you want to open.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:16.560
For example: a Friday release brief with what shipped, what is

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.800
blocked, and which decision needs you.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.720
Every item needs evidence.

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:24.720
Give the Bot six things: the outcome, inputs, scope,

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.400
evidence, stop point, and what to do when a source fails.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.120
Start with one repository and a small batch.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.240
Keep publishing and external messages behind your review.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.200
Now try a missing source, or a release without deployment

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.240
evidence. A useful Bot reports the gap.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.640
Once the method works on a second input, save it as a skill.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.620
Then choose whether it needs a routine.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.440
A Friday decision may not need an hourly sweep.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.760
Review which runs you actually use, narrow the sources, and

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.280
pause broken routines. This is a workflow recommendation, not

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.453
a measured claim about billing savings.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.400
My full guide includes three original starter briefs and a

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.000
TypeScript output check. Read it on my blog at B H J R dot

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.827
dev. A useful template gets you started.

00:01:06.853 --> 00:01:08.960
A clear assignment gets you a result.
